      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alvin%2C_Texas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Santa Fe Railroad built a flag station near Mustang Slough in the 1860s, sparking the first settlement that would become Alvin.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/alvin-tx",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Alvin’s early economy boomed with an ice factory, pickle works, cotton gin, bank, opera house, six hotels, four churches, and two weekly newspapers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/alvin-tx",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Alvin Library, part of the Brazoria County Library System, closed in 2010 after Hurricane Ike damage, reopened in June 2011, and now serves the community’s reading needs.",
